        "recipeIngredient": [
          "2/3 cup apple cider vinegar",
          "1/2 cup ketchup",
          "1/4 cup sugar",
          "2 teaspoons freshly ground black pepper",
          "2 teaspoons hot sauce, preferably Texas Pete",
          "1 large head green cabbage (about 3 1/2 pounds), finely shredded on a mandoline or by hand",
          "1 large carrot, peeled and grated on the large holes of a box grater",
          "2/3 cup sugar",
          "1/3 cup kosher salt"
        ],
        "recipeInstructions": [
          {
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "image": [
              {
                "@type": "ImageObject",
                "contentUrl": "https://www.seriouseats.com/thmb/hrYv4_7u3FP7Hjrfmc71KvTOZhE=/1500x0/filters:no_upscale():max_bytes(150000):strip_icc()/__opt__aboutcom__coeus__resources__content_migration__serious_eats__seriouseats.com__recipes__20140604-294803-lexington-red-slaw-step-1-157076a5751b49249a79c773ace90316.jpg",
                "creditText": "Serious Eats / Joshua Bousel"
              }
            ],
            "text": "For the Dressing: In a small bowl, whisk together vinegar, ketchup, sugar, black pepper, and hot sauce."
          },
          {
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "image": [
              {
                "@type": "ImageObject",
                "contentUrl": "https://www.seriouseats.com/thmb/c_kSpTZupEw-Fe2aF32jeclrSqo=/1500x0/filters:no_upscale():max_bytes(150000):strip_icc()/__opt__aboutcom__coeus__resources__content_migration__serious_eats__seriouseats.com__recipes__20140604-294803-slaw-step-2-2cc7c29b69334e83af55e8b96b2d25b9.jpg",
                "creditText": "Serious Eats / Joshua Bousel"
              }
            ],
            "text": "For the Slaw: Combine cabbage and carrot in a large bowl. Sprinkle with sugar and salt and toss to combine. Let stand 5 minutes, then transfer to a large colander and rinse thoroughly under cold running water."
          },
          {
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "image": [
              {
                "@type": "ImageObject",
                "contentUrl": "https://www.seriouseats.com/thmb/VUCA_kPcPBqgQQ31_vnx7brZAeE=/1500x0/filters:no_upscale():max_bytes(150000):strip_icc()/__opt__aboutcom__coeus__resources__content_migration__serious_eats__seriouseats.com__recipes__20140604-294803-slaw-step-3-92f99f96e9024a1eac5dc2ba6e7bd970.jpg",
                "creditText": "Serious Eats / Joshua Bousel"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Transfer vegetables to a salad spinner and spin dry. Alternatively, transfer to a large rimmed baking sheet lined with a triple layer of paper towels or a clean kitchen towel and blot mixture dry with more towels. Return to large bowl."
          },
          {
            "@type": "HowToStep",
            "image": [
              {
                "@type": "ImageObject",
                "contentUrl": "https://www.seriouseats.com/thmb/fKA-wPUyq0clIP5ZJPoNwE0PiAA=/1500x0/filters:no_upscale():max_bytes(150000):strip_icc()/__opt__aboutcom__coeus__resources__content_migration__serious_eats__seriouseats.com__recipes__20140604-294803-lexington-red-slaw-step-4-5f5ecde3d54e484ab14dc2bdfa117322.jpg",
                "creditText": "Serious Eats / Joshua Bousel"
              }
            ],
            "text": "Pour dressing over vegetables and toss to coat. Adjust seasoning to taste with salt, pepper, and/or sugar."
          }
        ],
        "recipeYield": "12",
        "review": [
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "WorthyLamb3687"
            },
            "datePublished": "2026-06-29T21:35:12.525Z",
            "reviewBody": "I make Lexington Style often. We lived 15 miles from Lexington, NC for our working careers, so are very used to eating it. I am intrigued by using tomato paste. I never asked the cooks at famous Lexington BBQ what they use. I will try it. In the mean time, I have been using chili sauce, a close cousin to ketchup with some spiciness. I use a LOT of Texas Pete, a must for red slaw and BBQ. Texas Pete is another NC product, made in Winston-Salem NC, close to Lexington. Garner Foods, owned by wonderful Garner family.",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mark H"
            },
            "datePublished": "2026-01-17T16:36:36.000Z",
            "reviewBody": "The real Lexington Style slaw doesn't use ketchup. That's a cheap imitation and shortcutting it changes the flavor. Tomato paste is the true way. Only non natives use ketchup."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "author": {
